# Vendored source provenance

gitree vendors part of the source of gitg. This file records what was
vendored and where it came from. It also gives the evidence that it is the
source of the gitg installed on the target platform.

Recorded: 2026-07-21, for gitrl-z, which vendors the same source.
Checked again: 2026-09-25, for gitree.

## Upstream

    Project:  gitg
    Version:  44
    URL:      https://download.gnome.org/sources/gitg/44/gitg-44.tar.xz
    SHA-256:  342a31684dab9671cd341bd3e3ce665adcee0460c2a081ddc493cdbc03132530
    Size:     10629268 bytes
    Licence:  GPL-2.0-or-later

`./fetch-upstream.sh` fetches the tarball again, verifies it and extracts it
into `vendor/upstream/`. That tree is not committed. It is 19 MB of code that
gitree does not change, and the checksum above makes it again on demand. The
repository holds the part cut from it, in `src/vendor-gitg/`, and each patch
to that part, in `vendor/patches/`.

## Target platform

    Distribution:  Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS (noble)
    Package:       gitg 44-1build2 (universe, amd64)
    Reported:      `gitg --version` -> "gitg 44"

On 2026-07-21 gitrl-z recorded Ubuntu 24.04.3. The gitg package is the same
on both dates.

## Does the vendored source match the installed gitg?

Yes, exactly. This was checked, not assumed, because the comparison of
gitree's window with gitg's depends on it.

Ubuntu's `gitg_44.orig.tar.xz` is not the same tarball as GNOME's
`gitg-44.tar.xz`. Ubuntu's tarballs are in
http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/pool/universe/g/gitg/.

    GNOME   gitg-44.tar.xz         10629268 bytes
            SHA-256 342a31684dab9671cd341bd3e3ce665adcee0460c2a081ddc493cdbc03132530

    Ubuntu  gitg_44.orig.tar.xz    10629352 bytes
            SHA-256 5b0e99ab3e7b94b0daa98ca8041d5ec9280ee0a2c28338a5506a968ac52e2354

Ubuntu cut its orig tarball from the upstream git history after the version
bump that follows the release, so its `meson.build` reads
`version: '45.alpha'`. Ubuntu then carries one patch, which reverts that
bump:

    debian/patches/series
      Revert-Bump-version-for-development.patch

A diff of the two extracted trees gives one file that differs:

    Files gnome/meson.build and ubuntu/meson.build differ
    3c3
    <   version: '44',
    ---
    >   version: '45.alpha',

When Ubuntu's patch is applied to its own orig tree, a diff against the
GNOME tarball gives no differences:

    $ patch -p1 < debian/patches/Revert-Bump-version-for-development.patch
    $ diff -rq gnome ubuntu-patched
    $ echo $?
    0

Thus GNOME's `gitg-44.tar.xz` is byte for byte the source that Ubuntu built
`gitg 44-1build2` from. It is correct to vendor from GNOME, and no Ubuntu
patch is necessary.

gitrl-z took this evidence on 2026-07-21. On 2026-09-25 each step was done
again for gitree, from fresh downloads of the three tarballs, and each result
above was the same.

## Ubuntu packaging tarball

`gitg_44-1build2.debian.tar.xz` (8036 bytes, SHA-256
253e380b9f89b270fb20ac34e056205e8de18e64a2d96b7d5a47eefc1c723a5a) is Ubuntu's
packaging for this version, kept for reference. Its `Build-Depends` lists
`libxml2-utils` and not `libxml2-dev`, so libxml2 is a tool at build time
(xmllint) and not a library that gitg links.

